# Who should own the firm?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Investors, workers, customers: why are most firms owned by capital? Why do cooperatives survive in some industries?"
next: {"title":"Why are some firms so much more productive than others?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/118-why-are-some-firms-so-much-more.cast"}

## Owned by whom?
sources:
    - id: coop
      title: 'Coop Norge: membership passes two million'
      authors: Coop Norge
      year: 2024
      finding: Coop's Norwegian co-operatives had over 2.1 million members at the end of 2023.
    - id: tine
      title: Tine (company)
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2025
      finding: 'TINE SA is a co-operative owned by its milk suppliers: 7,572 farmers in 2025.'
    - id: mondragon
      title: Mondragon Corporation
      authors: Wikipedia / Mondragon annual figures
      year: 2025
      finding: About 71,400 people work in the Mondragon group (2025), founded 1956 in the Basque Country.
    - id: rochdale
      title: Rochdale Society of Equitable Pioneers
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2025
      finding: Opened its store on Toad Lane, Rochdale, on 21 December 1844, selling butter, sugar, flour and oatmeal.
    - id: hansmann
      title: The Ownership of Enterprise
      authors: Henry Hansmann
      year: 1996
      finding: Ownership goes to the class of patrons that minimises the total costs of market contracting plus the costs of ownership.

    box firm "the firm" #3d3833 x 500 y 420 width 270 height 105 radius 14 icon ["factory"] icon_side left font_size 34 hidden true
    box inv "investors" #2f6b8f x 500 y 605 width 300 height 105 radius 14 icon ["money-bag"] icon_side left font_size 34 hidden true
    box cust "customers" #b5482e x 820 y 420 width 300 height 105 radius 14 icon ["shopping-basket"] icon_side left font_size 34 hidden true
    box work "workers" #b5482e x 180 y 420 width 300 height 105 radius 14 icon ["construction-worker"] icon_side left font_size 34 hidden true
    box supp "suppliers" #b5482e x 500 y 240 width 300 height 105 radius 14 icon ["cow"] icon_side left font_size 34 hidden true
    arrow a_inv inv -> firm thick #8f887c hidden true
    arrow a_cust cust -> firm thick #8f887c hidden true
    arrow a_work work -> firm thick #8f887c hidden true
    arrow a_supp supp -> firm thick #8f887c hidden true
    text t_roch "Rochdale shop, 1844" #8f887c cites ["rochdale"] x 820 y 322 font_size 28 hidden true
    text t_coop "Coop Norway:\n2+ million members" #b5482e cites ["coop"] x 820 y 308 font_size 28 hidden true
    text t_own "owner = control + what's left" #3d3833 x 250 y 322 font_size 28 hidden true
    text t_pat "patrons" #8f887c x 180 y 605 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Who owns the firm?"

Who should own the firm? In 1844, in Rochdale, in the north of England, a group of working men opened a shop owned by the people who shopped there.
    draw firm cust a_cust t_roch

B: Butter, sugar, flour and oatmeal. And the profit went back to the shoppers, in proportion to what each had bought.
    highlight cust

    erase t_roch

Their heirs are still trading. Coop in Norway has more than two million members, in a country of five and a half million.
    draw t_coop

B: So customers can own a firm. But they're only one of the candidates.
    draw inv a_inv work a_work supp a_supp parallel true

Owning a firm means holding two rights: to control it, and to keep what's left once everyone else is paid.
    draw t_own

Anyone who deals with the firm could hold them: investors, workers, customers, suppliers. The lawyer Henry Hansmann ca (@draw t_pat@)lls them its patrons.
    highlight inv work cust supp effect box

    erase t_pat

B: And each kind of patron owns somewhere. About seven and a half thousand dairy farmers own TINE, Norway's biggest dairy.
    text t_tine "TINE:\n~7,600 dairy farmers" #b5482e cites ["tine"] x 820 y 222 font_size 28

Some seventy thousand people work in the Mondragon group in the Basque Country, built from co-operatives their workers own.
    text t_mon "Mondragon: ~70,000" #b5482e cites ["mondragon"] x 180 y 518 font_size 28

B: Yet most big firms belong to  (@highlight inv effect circle@)the people who put in the money. Before we explain it, your guess.
    text t_most "most firms" #2f6b8f x 800 y 605 font_size 32

    quiz "Why do investors own most firms?" judge false voice b
        right: Hold that guess.
        * They bear the most risk
        * Money buys power
        * Tradition

Risk and power are the obvious answers. In 1996 Hansmann gave another, in a book called The Ownership of Enterprise.
    text t_hans "Hansmann, 1996" #3d3833 cites ["hansmann"] x 180 y 605 font_size 30

Ownership goes to whichever patrons can own most cheaply. Investors usually win, not because money rules, but because they all want the same thing.
    highlight inv effect pulse

    pause 0.5

## The cheapest owner
vars:
    d:
      value: 75
      min: 0
      max: 100
      label: Workers disagree (%)
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Investors own
      - Workers own
      - Customers own
    stacked: true
    series:
      - {name: contracting, values: [20, 20, 30]}
      - {name: owning, values: [10, 30, 40]}
    value_labels: true
    y_label: total cost
    y_ticks: true
    box:
      x: 30
      'y': 150
      w: 600
      h: 510
    label_size: 24
bind_params: {"series.1.values.1":"0.4*d"}
sources:
    - id: hansmann
      title: The Ownership of Enterprise
      authors: Henry Hansmann
      year: 1996
      finding: Ownership goes to the class of patrons that minimises the total costs of market contracting plus the costs of ownership; heterogeneous interests raise the cost of ownership.
    - id: statefarm
      title: State Farm
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2025
      finding: State Farm, a group of mutual insurers owned by policyholders, is the largest property-casualty and auto insurer in the US.
    - id: tine
      title: Tine (company)
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2025
      finding: TINE SA is a co-operative owned by its milk suppliers.

    scratch sum at.percent [78,62] work ["total cost =","contracting (non-owners)","+ owning (owners)"] font_size 28 hidden true
    text illus "illustrative" #8f887c x 560 y 640 font_size 24 hidden true
    text c_law "law firm:\npartners alike" #b5482e x 820 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true
    text c_mut "mutual insurer:\nState Farm" #b5482e cites ["statefarm"] x 820 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true
    text c_tine "farmer co-op:\nTINE" #b5482e cites ["tine"] x 820 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch mbox at.percent [81,58] work [{"tex":"C_j = \\text{own}_j + \\sum_{i \\ne j} \\text{contract}_i"},{"tex":"\\text{owner} = \\arg\\min_j C_j"},{"tex":"30 < 20 + 0.4 \\cdot 75 = 50"}] font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "The cheapest owner"

Cheapest at what? Hansmann adds up two costs. Take Harbourtown's boatyard, with illustrative numbers.
    draw axes

    draw illus

B: First, the cost of contracting: what the patrons who don't own lose by dealing with the firm only through contracts.
    draw sum_box sum_line_1 sum_line_2

A customer locked in can be overcharged; a worker with skills no one else needs can be squeezed.
    highlight sum_line_2

B: Second, the cost of owning: watching the managers, carrying the risk, and agreeing on what the firm should do.
    draw sum_line_3

    erase sum

Add both for each possible owner. If investors ow (@highlight bar_1 effect circle@)n the boatyard: thirty. Workers: fifty. Customers: seventy.
    draw bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 legend

B: Why are workers so dear as owners? Welders want safer docks, the young want higher pay, the old want better pensions.
    highlight bar_2

Every decision becomes a vote, and every vote a fight. Investors fight far less: each wants the most value for their share.
    highlight bar_1

B: Your turn: drag how much the workers disagree, and find the point where they become the cheapest owner.
    explore params ["d"]

Below about a quarter, workers win. Picture partners in a law firm: th (@draw c_law@)e same work, the same stakes. That's why lawyers own their own firms.
    animate d 15 duration 3 ghost true

    erase c_law

B: Customers win where they're alike and l (@highlight bar_3@)ocked in. Many big insurers, like State Farm in America, are owned by their policyholders.
    draw c_mut

    erase c_mut

And farmers own their dairy because otherwise they'd sell perishable milk to a single buyer who sets the price.
    draw c_tine

    erase c_tine

B: So the owner isn't capital by right. It's whoever makes the sum smallest, and tha (@highlight bar_1 effect circle@)t's usually capital.
    animate d 75 duration 2.5

    draw mathq

    ask "Hansmann's sum as a formula: open the math, or skip it?" on mathq store m default "Skip it" stage own voice b

@math

    erase mathq

For each candidate owner j: its own cost of owning, plus the contracting costs of every patron who doesn't own.
    draw mbox_box mbox_line_1

B: Then pick the owner with the smallest total.
    draw mbox_line_2

For the boatyard: investors thirty, workers twenty plus zero point four times seventy-five, which is fifty.
    draw mbox_line_3

    pause 0.5

    erase mbox

@test

    erase mathq

## Owners the sum can't explain
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Hansmann would make you a partner.
    good: You'd run a decent co-op.
    poor: Ask who is alike, and who is locked in.
    none: The firm will still need an owner tomorrow.
sources:
    - id: bennedsen
      title: 'Inside the Family Firm: The Role of Families in Succession Decisions and Performance'
      authors: Bennedsen, Nielsen, Pérez-González, Wolfenzon
      year: 2007
      finding: In Danish firms, using the sex of the departing CEO's first-born child as an instrument, family CEO successions cut operating return on assets by at least four percentage points (QJE 122(2)).
    - id: equinor
      title: Equinor
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2025
      finding: The Norwegian state owns 67% of Equinor.
    - id: codet
      title: Codetermination in Germany (1976 Codetermination Act; One-Third Participation Act)
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2025
      finding: 'Firms with over 2,000 employees: workers elect half the supervisory board; 500–2,000: one third.'
    - id: jsh
      title: Labor in the Boardroom
      authors: Simon Jäger, Benjamin Schoefer, Jörg Heining
      year: 2021
      finding: A 1994 reform abolished one-third worker board seats for stock corporations incorporated after August 1994; no effect on wages, rent sharing or the labour share; capital formation, if anything, rose (QJE 136(2)).
    - id: dow
      title: 'Governing the Firm: Workers'' Control in Theory and Practice'
      authors: Gregory K. Dow
      year: 2003
      finding: Labour-managed firms are rare partly because capital is alienable and labour is not; workers with little wealth struggle to finance firms.
    - id: ffpg
      title: 'Productivity, Capital, and Labor in Labor-Managed and Conventional Firms: An Investigation on French Data'
      authors: Fakhfakh, Pérotin, Gago
      year: 2012
      finding: French worker co-operatives are at least as productive as conventional firms (ILR Review 65(4)).
    - id: mondragon
      title: Mondragon Corporation
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2025
      finding: Mondragon set up its own credit co-operative, Caja Laboral, in 1959.

    text rule "owner = smallest sum" #8f887c x 815 y 625 font_size 34 hidden true
    box fam "Family heir takes over:\nROA −4 points (Denmark)" #3d3833 cites ["bennedsen"] width 560 height 125 radius 14 icon ["family"] icon_side left font_size 32 in col hidden true
    box st "State owns\n67% of Equinor" #3d3833 cites ["equinor"] width 560 height 125 radius 14 icon ["oil-drum"] icon_side left font_size 32 in col hidden true
    box de "Germany: workers elect\nhalf the board (2,000+ staff)" #3d3833 cites ["codet"] width 560 height 125 radius 14 icon ["handshake"] icon_side left font_size 32 in col hidden true
    column col gap 30 fit.h 480 fit.w 590 fit.x 30 fit.y 170 hidden true
    text little "little effect" #2f6b8f cites ["jsh"] x 820 y 250 font_size 34 hidden true
    text dow "Dow: workers\ncan't raise capital?" #b5482e cites ["dow"] x 820 y 560 font_size 28 hidden true
    text fr "co-ops at least as\nproductive (France)" #b5482e cites ["ffpg"] x 820 y 430 font_size 28 hidden true
    text s1 "ownership follows costs:\nmostly agreed" #4f7d3a x 815 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true
    text s2 "why worker firms\nare rare: contested" #b5482e x 815 y 320 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Owners the sum can't explain"

So ownership lands where the sum is smallest. But some owners don't fit the sum so neatly.
    draw rule

    fade rule to 0.5

B: Family firms, for one. In Denmark, Bennedsen and colleagues compared bosses whose first child was a son with those whose first child was a daughter.
    draw fam

Sons took over more often. And where a family heir took over, the return on assets fell by at least four percentage points.
    highlight fam

B: Then the state. Norway's government owns sixty-seven percent of Equinor. That's a choice about oil and politics, not about the cheapest patron, and not the natural-monopoly logic behind Statnett.
    draw st

And Germany mixes owners by law. In firms with over two thousand workers, employees elect half the supervisory board; in mid-sized ones, a third.
    draw de

B: Hansmann's sum says mixed control should be costly. Simon Jäger, Benjamin Schoefer and Jörg Heining tested it on a 1994 reform that ended the one-third seats for newly founded small companies.
    highlight rule

The seats changed little. No effect on wages, and if anything a bit more investment.
    draw little

B: And there's a rival reading. Maybe worker firms are rare not because they cost more to run, but because workers can't raise the money. That's Gregory Dow's case.
    draw dow

Mondragon fits it: it set up its own bank. And French studies find co-ops at least as productive as ordinary firms.
    draw fr

    erase rule little dow fr

How sure are we? That ownership follows these costs across industries: mostly agreed (@draw s2@). Why worker ownership is so rare: contested.
    draw s1

    hide col s1 s2

    cards sortc bins ["Investors","Workers","Customers","Suppliers"] deck true check each
        Workers: Accountants
        Suppliers: Grain co-op
        Investors: Shipping line
        Customers: Power co-op
        Workers: Dentists

    ask "Who most likely owns each: accountants' firm, grain co-op, shipping line, rural power co-op, dentists?" intro "Five quick cases." on sortc store f stage own voice b
        right: All five. Alike and locked in, the patrons own it; where big money must come first, investors do.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total}: only shipping is investors'.

    erase sortc

    show s1 s2

    quiz "A software start-up hires engineers, designers and salespeople and needs $100 million before it earns a cent. Who is the cheapest owner?"
        right: Investors: the workers are a mixed crowd who would disagree, and the money has to come first.
        wrong: Mixed workers disagree, and the money must come first: here, investors.
        + Investors
        * Its workers
        * Its first customers

    quiz "A taxi co-op's drivers all work the same shifts in the same city, and each owns a car. Would Hansmann expect it to survive as worker-owned?" voice b
        right: Yes: alike owners who want the same thing can run it cheaply.
        wrong: Ask whether the drivers want the same thing: here, yes, it can survive.
        + Yes: the owners are alike
        * No: investors always win
        * Only with state help

    show col

B: So who should own the firm? Whoever can own it most cheaply. Usually that's capital; where workers or customers are alike and locked in, it's them.
    highlight s1 s2 effect box

Next: why, whoever owns them, some firms are so much more productive than others.
    highlight s1

