    '|a||And Germany mixes owners by law. In firms with over two thousand workers, employees elect half the supervisory board; in mid-sized ones, a third.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8520
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:2q2r2e'
    '|b||Hansmann''s sum says mixed control should be costly. Simon Jäger, Benjamin Schoefer and Jörg Heining tested it on a 1994 reform that ended the one-third seats for newly founded small companies.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12800
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:de7n6w'
    '|a||The seats changed little. No effect on wages, and if anything a bit more investment.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5080
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:kuqqa3'
    '|b||And there''s a rival reading. Maybe worker firms are rare not because they cost more to run, but because workers can''t raise the money. That''s Gregory Dow''s case.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9320
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:bt66jh'
    '|a||Mondragon fits it: it set up its own bank. And French studies find co-ops at least as productive as ordinary firms.':
